WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.177
Readable code is not only about variable names.

00:00:03.177 --> 00:00:07.047
It is also about style, comments, and formatting.

00:00:07.047 --> 00:00:12.572
Spacing helps separate ideas. Blank lines can show where one step ends and another step begins.

00:00:12.572 --> 00:00:16.657
Comments are also important, but we need to use them carefully.

00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:21.192
A comment should not simply repeat what the code already says.

00:00:21.192 --> 00:00:24.532
A useful comment explains why a decision was made.

00:00:24.532 --> 00:00:26.652
Look at the example on this slide.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:29.122
We have a small list of order records.

00:00:29.122 --> 00:00:33.182
Some orders are complete and one order is cancelled.

00:00:33.182 --> 00:00:40.637
The comment explains that cancelled orders are excluded because they did not produce revenue.

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:42.322
That is a business rule.

00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:45.332
It explains the reason for the filtering step.

00:00:45.332 --> 00:00:49.482
The code then filters completed orders and calculates revenue.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.807
Run the cell and notice the output.

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:53.562
Then think about the comment.

00:00:53.562 --> 00:00:59.177
If someone reads this code later, the comment helps them understand the logic behind the decision.

00:00:59.177 --> 00:01:05.887
A practical rule is this: write comments for decisions, assumptions, and unusual logic.

00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:08.776
Do not write a comment for every obvious line.
